**ARTICLE VIII: SAFETY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM**

**8.01. Implementation of Safety Management System (SMS)**  
GAT shall establish a Safety Management System (SMS) in compliance with FAA regulations and ICAO standards. This system will encompass all operational aspects of GAT’s activities, including flight operations, maintenance, and emergency response. The SMS shall be designed to proactively identify hazards, assess risks, and implement safety measures.

**8.02. Safety Reporting System**  
GAT, in collaboration with AMRO and AeroSafe, shall maintain a confidential safety reporting system that encourages employees to report safety concerns without fear of reprisal. Reports shall be reviewed regularly to identify trends and areas for improvement in GAT’s operational safety.

**8.03. Safety Training and Awareness**  
All personnel involved in operations, maintenance, and management shall receive regular training on the SMS and associated safety protocols. This training shall include emergency procedures, risk management practices, and compliance with regulatory requirements. GAT shall collaborate with IAA to ensure that training programs meet industry best practices.

**ARTICLE IX: ENVIRONMENTAL MANAGEMENT**

**9.01. Environmental Compliance**  
GAT shall adhere to all applicable federal, state, and local environmental laws and regulations. This includes, but is not limited to, emissions standards, waste disposal regulations, and noise abatement procedures. GAT shall work closely with the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to ensure compliance.

**9.02. Sustainability Initiatives**  
GAT, along with its partners, shall develop and implement sustainability initiatives aimed at reducing the environmental impact of its operations. These initiatives may include the adoption of fuel-efficient aircraft, the use of sustainable aviation fuels (SAF), and waste reduction strategies.

**9.03. Environmental Audits**  
GAT shall conduct regular environmental audits to assess compliance with environmental regulations and the effectiveness of sustainability initiatives. Results of these audits shall be shared with IAA and made available to stakeholders upon request.

**ARTICLE X: COLLABORATION AND COMMUNICATION**

**10.01. Stakeholder Engagement**  
GAT shall engage with relevant stakeholders, including regulatory bodies, community representatives, and industry partners, to foster open communication and collaboration on issues related to safety, operations, and environmental impact.

**10.02. Joint Safety Committees**  
A Joint Safety Committee shall be established, comprising representatives from GAT, AMRO, AeroSafe, IAA, and FOMG. This committee shall meet quarterly to discuss safety performance, review safety reports, and collaborate on safety improvement initiatives.

**10.03. Information Sharing**  
GAT and its partners shall create an information-sharing platform to facilitate the exchange of safety data, operational insights, and best practices. This platform shall enhance collaboration and promote a culture of safety and accountability across all entities involved in GAT’s operations.

**ARTICLE XI: FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITIES**

**11.01. Cost Allocation**  
The parties agree to allocate costs associated with maintenance, safety training, and environmental compliance as follows: GAT shall cover all operational costs, while AMRO shall bear the costs of routine maintenance services. AeroSafe shall be responsible for costs related to the implementation of safety protocols.

**11.02. Insurance Requirements**  
GAT shall maintain comprehensive insurance coverage, including liability, hull, and cargo insurance, with limits satisfactory to both AeroSafe and IAA. Proof of insurance shall be provided to the parties on an annual basis or upon request.

**11.03. Financial Audits**  
Annual financial audits shall be conducted by an independent auditor to assess compliance with the financial terms outlined in this Agreement. The results of these audits shall be shared with all parties to ensure transparency and accountability.

